% Problem 36. Chace vol. II pl. 58 (photograph XV, registers 1-2), PDF pp. 163-164; Peet pp. 72-74 (Pl. L).
No. 36
"Go down I times 3, '3 of me, '5 of me is added to me; return I, filled am I. What is the quantity saying it?"
  1      1
  1      1
  1      1
  '3     '3
  '5     '5
% no total is written (Peet: the sum 3 '3 '5 = 106/30 "is suppressed")
  1      106
  '2     53
\ '4     26 '2
\ '106   1
\ '53    2
\ '212   '2
Total 1.               % the ticked products add to 30; Peet: "the scribe, instead of giving the total of the products as 30, gives it as 1. This must not be regarded as a mere error"; Chace prints dmd 1 without comment
% the proof (no heading): the answer '4 '53 '106 '212 times 1, 2, '3, '5
  1      '4 53 '106 '212           % 53 for '53: Chace fn 1: "The scribe omitted the fractional dots in these places."
  2      '2 '30 '318 '795 '53 '106 % Peet: 2 x '53 resolved by the table into '30 '318 '795
  '3     '12 '159 '318 '636
  '5     '20 '265 '530 '1060
% set out again with the red values, in 1060ths, beneath each fraction ('4 and '2 left out); each line's red sum in red at the right
"53 '106 '212"                    % 53 for '53 (Chace fn 1)
"{20 10 5}"
"{35}"
"30 '318 '795 '53 '106"           % 30 for '30 (Chace fn 1)
"{35 '3 3 '3 1 '3 '20 10}"        % '20: Chace fn 2: "These are whole numbers, but are marked with fractional dots in the original" (20 written as '20)
"{70}"
"'12 '159 '318 '636"              % '12: Chace fn 3: "The fractional dot here is made like a dash in the hieratic, giving the number the appearance of 22"
"{88 '3 6 2/3 3 '3 1 2/3}"
"{100}"
"'20 '265 '530 '1060"
"{53 4 2 1}"
"{80}"                            % Chace fn 4: "Mistake for 60"; Peet: "80 (read 60)"
"'4 '265"                         % '265: Chace fn 2: a whole number (265) marked with a fractional dot; the four red sums (35 70 100 60) make 265, '4 of 1060
  '2     530
  '4     265
  '4     265
Total 1060.            % Peet: '2 and '4 (the fractions left out above) and the '4 just found, in 1060ths
